      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5bb7bf9e2297f24ce1cca4f4|full_name}: Fog in Unity is only a visual effect. \nThe main cost to performance in worlds is from draw calls. Unity's cameras have a far plane option at which everything after is not rendered. This is controlled by the world creator. Anything inside this value is fully rendered, no matter how much you see, unless the occlusion culling system also decides it is behind something and can't be seen. Occlusion culling also decides whether the background objects can be not drawn.\nIf a world is well optimised then lowering the render distance would have no effect on performance until you could not see more than a few feet in front of you, because a well optimised world would reuse materials to save on draw calls. If a world is not well optimised then it will make no difference, because part of optimisation is setting up the occlusion culling system that allows Unity to clip distant things out to begin with. If a world lies in the middle of this criteria then it still would not help unless the map was full of many small, intricate objects spread across a wide area.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
